1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,829
Today, I want to talk about self-regulatory focus theory

2
00:00:04,829 --> 00:00:12,340
and how the actions of leaders can affect the way followers approach different situations.

3
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:18,320
Self-regulatory focus theory is a theory developed by Tori Higgins.

4
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:27,720
He says that a person's focus at any given time is to either approach pleasure or avoid pain.

5
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:33,050
These are two basic motivations that each and every one of us has,

6
00:00:33,050 --> 00:00:37,150
and they cause us to have different kinds of goals.

7
00:00:37,150 --> 00:00:43,540
Promotion goals in different life situations emphasise achievement.

8
00:00:43,540 --> 00:00:50,430
Prevention goals are oriented towards the avoidance of punishment.

9
00:00:50,430 --> 00:01:01,760
In a specific situation, our thoughts might focus more on promotion goals or more on prevention goals.

10
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:08,660
The theory suggests that two factors affect which goals we are focusing on

11
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:12,000
First, there is a chronic factor.

12
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,015
This factor is connected to a person's personality

13
00:01:16,015 --> 00:01:20,040
and says that each person has a basic tendency

14
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:30,570
to either focus more on promotion goals or focus more on prevention goals as part of his or her personality.

15
00:01:30,570 --> 00:01:33,293
Second, there is a situational factor

16
00:01:33,293 --> 00:01:43,730
which means that the context we are in can make us more likely to focus on one set of goals or the other.

17
00:01:43,730 --> 00:01:48,350
For example, we are more likely to be thinking about pleasure

18
00:01:48,350 --> 00:01:53,810
and to have promotion goals when we are spending time with a friend.

19
00:01:53,810 --> 00:02:00,322
In contrast, if we are working on an important project for our boss,

20
00:02:00,322 --> 00:02:04,860
we are more likely to try to avoid making mistakes

21
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:09,840
and therefore have more prevention goals in our mind.

22
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:19,250
Research has shown that the goals we are focusing on at a given time affect the way we think.

23
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:23,860
For example, when focusing on promotion goals

24
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:29,730
people consider their ideal self, their aspirations and gains.

25
00:02:29,730 --> 00:02:36,330
They don't think about what they can lose, so they think in a happier mode.

26
00:02:36,330 --> 00:02:39,520
They feel more inspired to change.

27
00:02:39,520 --> 00:02:46,300
When people are focusing on prevention goals, they think about their "ought" self.

28
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:48,900
What are they supposed to be?

29
00:02:48,900 --> 00:02:51,550
What are people expecting from them?

30
00:02:51,550 --> 00:02:55,400
They consider their obligations to others.

31
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:59,340
As a result, they experience more anxiety

32
00:02:59,340 --> 00:03:08,530
and try to avoid situations where they could lose.

33
00:03:08,530 --> 00:03:15,007
Now that I have talked about the two focuses and how they affect people,

34
00:03:15,007 --> 00:03:20,558
I want to look at the idea that the way leaders behave,

35
00:03:20,558 --> 00:03:20,620
or their style of leading

36
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:27,960
can affect the focus that followers adopt in a specific situation.

37
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:37,340
In talking about leadership, we often mention transformational leaders and transactional leaders.

38
00:03:37,340 --> 00:03:44,960
Transformational leaders, when interacting with their followers, focus on their development.

39
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:51,260
In their words and actions transformational leaders highlight change.

40
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:57,200
Their speech is passionate and conveys a definitive vision.

41
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:03,710
All of these things can encourage followers to think about what could be.

42
00:04:03,710 --> 00:04:10,910
In other words, they inspire a promotion focus in their followers.

43
00:04:10,910 --> 00:04:23,670
In contrast, transactional leaders focus on developing clear structures that tell their followers exactly what is expected of them.

44
00:04:23,670 --> 00:04:29,495
While they do explain the rewards people will get for following orders,

45
00:04:29,495 --> 00:04:37,020
they emphasise more how a follower will be punished or that a follower won't get rewarded

46
00:04:37,020 --> 00:04:40,660
if his or her behaviour doesn't change.

47
00:04:40,660 --> 00:04:46,580
In short, they emphasise the consequences of making a mistake.

48
00:04:46,580 --> 00:04:54,430
This emphasis will clearly lead followers to focus on avoiding punishment and problems.

49
00:04:54,430 --> 00:04:58,920
This is clearly a prevention focus.

50
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:07,640
In conclusion, it is important to understand that one focus is not necessarily better than the other one.

51
00:05:07,640 --> 00:05:13,900
For a designer who works in a field where a lot of innovation is needed

52
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:17,330
a promotion focus is probably better.

53
00:05:17,330 --> 00:05:23,082
In contrast, a prevention focus which causes people to work more cautiously

54
00:05:23,082 --> 00:05:31,230
and produce higher quality work might be very appropriate for a job like a surgeon, for example.

55
00:05:31,230 --> 00:05:34,234
The main point of the research,

56
00:05:34,234 --> 00:05:39,380
though, is that the actions of leaders can greatly influence

57
00:05:39,380 --> 00:05:41,743
whether people approach a situation

58
00:05:41,743 --> 00:05:47,750
with more of a promotion focus or more of a prevention focus.
